IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `bda-debt-agreements`
IMPORT `bda-bankruptcy`

-- Bankruptcy Act 1966 (Cth), what bankruptcy would mean (ss 139K, 139P, 149, 149A, 153) -- worked cases from the debtor's seat.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Nothing here has been checked against a
-- decided case. Amounts set by the Bankruptcy Regulations 2021 (s 10A, ss 36, 82, 83) are taken from the deposited compilation.
--
-- Fixtures are generated from a table of which flags are set (scratchpad gen_bankruptcy-act-1966_baseline.py). Expected results were written by hand from the
-- text.
--
-- Scenario facts used (workbench scenarios/au/wa/cradle-to-grave-jake/facts.json and SCENARIO.md): Jake Harding, born 2006-08-30;
-- debts of $41,000 and collectors (k064, 2037-02-06); a creditor's petition threatened and a debt agreement proposed instead,
-- accepted by a majority of creditors in value (k065, 2037-04-20); "A five-year plan: $690 a month to the administrator" (k066,
-- 2037-06-01); the agreement completes (k075, 2042-05-31). From 2033-11-01 he rents a unit in Gosnells under the Residential
-- Tenancies Act 1987 (WA); he owns no home in 2037. The scenario gives the threshold as "indexed; about $150,000 in unsecured
-- debts": that figure is the scenario's, used as an INPUT. ASSUMED here, not in the scenario: the agreement is made on 2037-06-01;
-- no divisible property; a likely after tax income of $52,000; the proposal given 3 days after signing; the split of the $41,000
-- among creditors (illustrative only); and, for bankruptcy, an assessed income of $65,000 and an illustrative base income
-- threshold of $70,000.

§ `ss 149, 149A -- how long I would be bankrupt`

-- k065: the alternative Jake avoids. Bankrupt on a debtor's petition accepted 2037-05-01, he would be discharged 2040-05-01.
#ASSERT `How many years would I be bankrupt` `no objection in effect` EQUALS 3
#ASSERT `How many years would I be bankrupt` `an objection on a ground listed in s 149A(2)(a)(i)` EQUALS 8

#ASSERT `When would I be discharged` 20370501 `no objection in effect` EQUALS 20400501

§ `s 153 -- what discharge would release`

#ASSERT `Would discharge release me from the debt` `an ordinary provable debt` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Would discharge release me from the debt` `a debt incurred by fraud` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Would discharge release me from the debt` `a liability under a maintenance agreement or maintenance order` FALSE
#ASSERT `Would discharge release me from the debt` `a liability under a maintenance agreement or maintenance order` TRUE

§ `ss 139K, 139P -- income contributions`

-- k065/k066: in 2037 Ruby (4) lives with Jake and has no income; she is his dependant. Tahlia earns, so is not (her income is
-- assumed above the limit). Regulations s 36: $3,741 for a period beginning in 2020-21.
#ASSERT `Is this person my dependant` TRUE TRUE 0 3741
#ASSERT NOT `Is this person my dependant` TRUE TRUE 30000 3741


#ASSERT `What is my actual income threshold amount` 70000 1 EQUALS 82600
#ASSERT `What is my actual income threshold amount` 70000 6 EQUALS 95200

-- Jake, assessed at $65,000 with one dependant against the illustrative $70,000 base, would owe no contribution.
#ASSERT NOT `Would I have to pay an income contribution` 65000 70000 1
#ASSERT `Would I have to pay an income contribution` 90000 70000 1

#ASSERT `How much does my income exceed the threshold` 90000 70000 1 EQUALS 7400

-- s 139S, transcribed from the Word rendering (C1966A00033.formulas.md): (assessed income - actual income threshold amount) / 2.
#ASSERT `How much income contribution would I pay` 90000 70000 1 EQUALS 3700
#ASSERT `How much income contribution would I pay` 100000 70000 0 EQUALS 15000

